"""Golden characterization net for the health baseline / variations pipeline.

Pins the EXACT output of ``HealthMetricsService.compute_kind_baseline_delta``
and ``HealthMetricsService.detect_all_variations`` for 15 datasets crossed with
every ``window_days`` an LLM-facing tool can request (1..14 — the clamp of
``HEALTH_METRICS_BASELINE_WINDOW_MAX_DAYS``), BEFORE the per-sample fetch is
replaced by a server-side per-day rollup (``fetch_daily_stats``). Method:
characterization-first decomposition (Feathers), as applied by ADR-122 for the
voice/TTS extraction and by extraction #2 for the draft preview renderer — this
net must pass identically, UNMODIFIED, after the conversion.

Why this exact shape:

- **The oracle cannot be the sample-based function.** After the conversion the
  sample-based entry points become thin wrappers over the stats-based ones, so
  comparing the two would be tautological. The table below is therefore
  generated by RUNNING the pre-conversion implementation and pinned as
  literals — never written by hand, regenerated ONLY on a deliberate, reviewed
  behavior change with the old/new tables diffed.
- **One dataset, both read paths.** ``_FakeSampleRepo`` serves BOTH repository
  interfaces (``fetch_samples_kind`` today, ``fetch_daily_stats`` after) from a
  single in-memory source of truth, so the very same net exercises the old and
  the new read path without a single edit. What the unit net proves is that the
  service contract is invariant across the two paths; that the SQL rollup
  equals the in-Python grouping is pinned separately by the integration test
  ``test_daily_stats_rollup.py`` (real PostgreSQL).
- **The clock is pinned per module.** ``detect_notable_events`` branches on
  ``datetime.now(UTC)``; without a frozen clock the table would be a calendar
  time bomb (the failure mode documented in ``test_context_aggregator.py``).
  Patching is scoped to the modules under test — no global freeze.

Branch coverage encoded in the case table (counted at generation time):

- Baseline modes: ``empty`` (72 entries), ``bootstrap`` (14), ``rolling`` (124).
- Notable variations: 36, both directions (``rising`` 12, ``falling`` 24).
- Notable events: 24 ``inactivity_streak`` occurrences.
- Both registered kinds: ``heart_rate`` (``DAILY_AVG``) and ``steps``
  (``DAILY_SUM``) — i.e. every ``BaselineKind`` reachable from the registry.
- Window boundaries: 1 (degenerate — window is the whole series), 6/7/8 (the
  bootstrap↔rolling switch at ``health_metrics_baseline_min_days``), 14 (the
  tool clamp).
- Data shapes: empty, missing days (gaps), monotonic rise, monotonic fall,
  all-zero values (null baseline → early return), steps inactivity streak,
  constant series (no variation).
- Fixture clock semantics — read this before adding a case: samples are placed
  at 12:00 UTC of their day while the frozen clock reads 11:00, so a
  ``days_ago=0`` entry falls *after* ``now`` and is excluded by the
  ``date_start < to_ts`` bound. The cases named ``*_after_now`` /
  ``*_last_in_future`` therefore pin that exclusion, and are NOT same-day
  coverage. Genuine same-day behaviour (``summary_today``,
  ``last_update_minutes_ago``) is covered by the heartbeat builder net, and a
  single-visible-day series by ``test_single_visible_day_*`` below — written as
  explicit assertions rather than golden entries, because the table must stay
  the one generated before the conversion.
- Non-terminating daily averages (3 and 7 samples/day whose sum is not
  divisible by the count). Without them the net is numerically blind: every
  other dataset yields a daily average of ``.0`` or ``.5``, so a rounding
  drift stays invisible.

Sensitivity verified by mutation testing (the net is worthless if it only
passes): ``DAILY_AVG`` → ``min`` fails 56 entries; the window split boundary
``<`` → ``<=`` fails 136; rounding the daily average to 1 decimal fails 14 —
that last one is caught ONLY by ``EXPECTED_DAILY`` plus the two
repeating-decimal cases, since the service payload rounds everything to one
decimal and hides sub-0.05 drift.
"""

